<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southern Netherlands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> were the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> that remained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>, corresponding roughly to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. Ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> until 1714 and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, they were also called the Spanish and Austrian Netherlands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> garrisoned barrier fortresses there after 1715, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the region in 1795.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Southern Netherlands, also called the Catholic Netherlands, were the parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> that came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule. They covered most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, small parts of the modern Netherlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and until 1678 most of the present Nord-Pas-de-Calais region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Several small states in the region were never ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>: the Prince-Bishopric of Liège, the Imperial Abbey of Stavelot-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a>, the County of Bouillon, the County of Horne and the Princely Abbey of Thorn. The capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, lay in Brabant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> were wealthy and led by a merchant class, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> attempts to raise taxes for their wars, together with penal laws that made heresy a capital crime, provoked a rebellion that began the Eighty Years' War. The seven northern provinces, led by Holland and Zeeland, established their independence as the United Provinces after 1581, while Alexander Farnese, Duke of Parma, reconquered the ten southern ones. In the early 17th century the Archduchess Isabella and her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-vii-archduke-of-austria">Archduke Albert</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> governed from a flourishing court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, from which the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens">Peter Paul Rubens</a> emerged. Albert's death in 1621 returned the territory to formal Spanish control.</p><p>As Spanish power waned, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took a growing share. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659 it annexed Artois, and the treaties of Aix-la-Chapelle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> later added Cambrai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloon-flanders">Walloon Flanders</a> and half of Hainaut. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rastatt">Treaty of Rastatt</a> in 1714 gave what was left to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, largely at British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> insistence, since they feared French domination. Barrier fortresses along the border were garrisoned with Dutch troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Joseph II's centralizing policies provoked a rebellion in 1788, and the provinces formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in January 1790. His brother and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> restored Austrian rule by the end of that year. After the Battle of Sprimont in 1794 French armies overran the region, which was annexed to the French Republic in 1795. Most of the population was hostile to French rule, above all because of conscription, the assignat and the Republic's anti-religious policies: in 1797 nearly 8,000 priests refused an oath and went into hiding.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> confirmed its loss at Campo Formio in 1797. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> of 1815 gave the region to the new United Kingdom of the Netherlands, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> of 1830 it became the independent Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Southern Netherlands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
